    <exercises xml:id="exercises-laws-of-probability">
        <exercise xml:id="ex-juice-kiosk-orders">
            <statement><p>At a juice kiosk in Nakuru, each customer orders one juice. A customer orders mango juice with probability <m>0.25</m> and passion juice with probability <m>0.4</m>; every other customer orders avocado juice. Find (a) <m>P(\text{mango or passion})</m>, (b) <m>P(\text{avocado})</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>0.65</m> (b) <m>0.35</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-alarm-and-matatu">
            <statement><p>The probability that Otieno's alarm fails is <m>0.05</m> and the probability that his matatu is late is <m>0.2</m>, independently. Find the probability that (a) both happen, (b) neither happens.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>0.01</m> (b) <m>0.76</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p>(a) <m>0.05 \times 0.2 = 0.01</m>. (b) <m>0.95 \times 0.8 = 0.76</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-coin-and-die-laws">
            <statement><p>A coin is tossed and a die is rolled. Find the probability of (a) a tail and an odd number, (b) a head and a 6. Check your answers by listing the outcomes.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>\frac{1}{4}</m> (b) <m>\frac{1}{12}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-two-dice-laws">
            <statement><p>Two dice are rolled. Find the probability that (a) both show an even number, (b) the first shows 1 and the second shows 6.</p></statement>
            <answer><p>(a) <m>\frac{1}{4}</m> (b) <m>\frac{1}{36}</m></p></answer>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-find-the-other-probability">
            <statement><p><m>A</m> and <m>B</m> are independent, <m>P(A) = 0.3</m> and <m>P(A \text{ and } B) = 0.12</m>. Find <m>P(B)</m>.</p></statement>
            <answer><p><m>0.4</m></p></answer>
            <solution><p><m>0.3 \times P(B) = 0.12</m>, so <m>P(B) = 0.12 \div 0.3 = 0.4</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise xml:id="ex-kioko-weekend-rain">
            <title>Challenge: A Wet Weekend?</title>
            <statement><p>Kioko says: <q>The probability of rain on Saturday is <m>0.6</m> and on Sunday is <m>0.6</m>, so the probability of rain at the weekend is <m>1.2</m>.</q> What is wrong? Assuming the two days are independent, find the probability of rain on at least one of the two days.</p></statement>
            <hint><p>First find the probability of no rain on either day.</p></hint>
            <answer><p>The events are not mutually exclusive (it can rain on both days), and no probability can be <m>1.2</m>. The probability is <m>0.84</m>.</p></answer>
            <solution><p>P(no rain on either day) <m>= 0.4 \times 0.4 = 0.16</m>, so P(rain on at least one day) <m>= 1 - 0.16 = 0.84</m>.</p></solution>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Differentiation</title>
        <table xml:id="table-differentiation-laws-of-probability">
            <title>Differentiation Strategies</title>
            <tabular halign="left" top="minor" bottom="minor" left="minor" right="minor">
                <row header="yes" bottom="medium"><cell>Learner Group</cell><cell>Strategy and Learning Experience</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Struggling Learners (Support)</cell><cell>Give a two-row card: <q>or, and cannot both happen: add</q>; <q>and, and do not affect each other: multiply</q>. Focus on <xref ref="example-getting-to-school"/> and <xref ref="example-coin-and-die-law"/>. Assign <xref ref="ex-juice-kiosk-orders"/> and <xref ref="ex-coin-and-die-laws"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>On-Level Learners (Core)</cell><cell>Full activity and all examples. Assign <xref ref="ex-juice-kiosk-orders"/> to <xref ref="ex-find-the-other-probability"/>.</cell></row>
                <row><cell>Advanced Learners (Challenge)</cell><cell>All exercises including <xref ref="ex-kioko-weekend-rain"/>. Extension: is <q>it rains on Saturday</q> really independent of <q>it rains on Sunday</q>? (Probably not: wet spells often last several days, so the model is a simplification.)</cell></row>
            </tabular>
        </table>
    </axiom>


